# Gestion Annonces Code Style

- Use explicit, business-oriented names that match the module spec.
- Keep class and file names aligned with the domain entities and services: `ResourceListing`, `PostAttachment`, `Comment`, `GroupPurchase`, `GroupParticipant`, `Favorite`.
- Prefer one clear responsibility per class.
- Controller names should end with `Controller`.
- Service classes should end with `Service`; implementation classes may end with `ServiceImpl` only if the project already uses that convention.
- Repository names should end with `Repository`.
- DTO names should be explicit, such as `CreateListingRequest`, `ListingResponse`, `JoinGroupRequest`, `CommentResponse`.
- Mapper names should end with `Mapper`.
- WebSocket-related classes should clearly indicate purpose, such as `ListingWebSocketController` or `GroupNotificationPublisher`.
- Use enums for listing type, listing status, and group status instead of string literals.
- Keep REST endpoints resource-oriented and aligned with the finalized spec.
- Keep validation close to request DTOs and enforce business validation again in services.
- Avoid bloated controllers. Put business rules in services, persistence in repositories, and transport mapping in mappers/DTOs.
- Keep methods short and purposeful. Split large flows into helper methods when they represent meaningful business steps.
- Prefer readable code over clever abstractions.
- Do not introduce chat-related naming or classes; use comment/thread terminology only.
- Do not introduce extra domain entities unless the spec is explicitly extended.
